@meta
id: 18-01
title: Open a file by its path
chapter: 18
type: lesson
skills: edit-file
par: 63
minutes: 4
vscode: Ctrl+P, type the name, Enter
open: README.md

@brief
You are in the README. It says the order statuses are `PENDING`,
`SHIPPED` and `CANCELLED`. The entity file disagrees: it spells the last
one `CANCELED`, with one L, and the GraphQL schema has been rejecting it
since Tuesday.

The README also tells you where entities live. Open the order entity from
inside vim, fix the spelling, and save. Do not touch the README.

Paths are relative to the directory vim was started in, which is the
project root here. `:pwd` shows it if you are ever unsure.

Type the path in full this time. It will feel long. That is the point of
the next lesson.

@keys
| `:e path` | edit a file — open it, creating it if it does not exist |
| `:pwd` | the directory relative paths are resolved from |
| `:e!` | throw away changes and reload the current file |

@hints
The README's layout section gives the path. Read it, then `:e` it.
--
`:e src/domain/order/order.entity.ts` and Enter. Then search for the
misspelling rather than scrolling to it.
--
`/CANCELED` puts you on the word. `fL` finds the L, `aL` adds a second
one, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    :e src/domain/order/order.entity.ts<Enter>
    /CANCELED<Enter> fL aL <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`:e` is the whole of this lesson. It takes a path, relative to `:pwd`,
and makes that file the current buffer. If the file does not exist you
get an empty buffer with that name, and it becomes real the first time
you write it. Everything chapter 16 taught about buffers applies: the
README is still loaded, `:ls` lists both, `:bp` goes back.

Thirty-six keystrokes of path is a lot, and you will not do it this way
for long. The next four lessons are about never typing a full path again.

@start README.md
# Orders service

Handles orders, customers and refunds for the storefront.

## Layout

    src/domain/<name>/<name>.entity.ts       the shape of a record
    src/domain/<name>/<name>.repository.ts   how it is loaded and saved
    src/domain/<name>/<name>.provider.ts     the business rules
    src/infrastructure/services/             calls to other systems
    src/configuration/errors/default.json    every error code we can throw

## Order statuses

An order is `PENDING` until it ships, `SHIPPED` once the carrier has it,
and `CANCELLED` if the customer or an operator stops it first.

@start src/domain/order/order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELED';

@expected src/domain/order/order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@start src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}
